1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,860
Could a dying star explode near our solar
system and rip Earth's atmosphere away

2
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:05,660
into space?

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:11,500
Do science fiction movies show a fiery
blast wave sweeping our planet bare within

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:12,300
seconds?

5
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:16,220
Why does this question matter when
considering terrestrial life?

6
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:19,900
How essential is our oxygen
atmosphere for survival?

7
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:25,600
In reality, a supernova is a powerful
explosion marking the death of a star.

8
00:00:26,394 --> 00:00:28,280
When a massive star runs out of fuel,

9
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:31,920
its core collapses under
immense gravitational force.

10
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:37,720
Alternatively, an exploding white dwarf
star undergoes runaway nuclear fusion.

11
00:00:38,453 --> 00:00:43,620
These stellar detonations expel several
solar masses of matter into the surrounding

12
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:44,880
interstellar medium.

13
00:00:45,641 --> 00:00:50,660
The ejected material travels outward at
speeds reaching several per cent of the

14
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:51,460
speed of light.

15
00:00:51,948 --> 00:00:56,940
This creates an expanding shock wave that
sweeps up a shell of interstellar gas and

16
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:57,740
dust.

17
00:00:58,944 --> 00:01:02,533
Where are the nearest stars that might
explode in our stellar neighbourhood?

18
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:08,100
Astronomers define a near-Earth supernova
as one occurring within roughly 33

19
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:10,695
to 978 light-years.

20
00:01:11,144 --> 00:01:16,180
Currently, our galaxy holds up to 14
potential near-Earth supernovas inside

21
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:17,440
that broader distance.

22
00:01:18,225 --> 00:01:23,240
A few famous stars located within several
hundred light-years could explode in just

23
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,300
1,000 years.

24
00:01:25,050 --> 00:01:27,360
Although these dying stars
will shine brilliantly,

25
00:01:28,180 --> 00:01:30,320
they pose little direct
threat to our planet.

26
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:34,328
What about the famous red
supergiant star Betelgeuse?

27
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:39,920
Scientists estimate that Betelgeuse
could require as long as 1.5 million

28
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:42,080
years to explode as a supernova.

29
00:01:42,857 --> 00:01:47,700
When it detonates, its particle stream
will reach Earth about 100,000 years

30
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:49,260
after the initial light.

31
00:01:49,954 --> 00:01:54,860
Looking back in time, other relatively
nearby stars have exploded in Earth's past.

32
00:01:55,651 --> 00:02:00,400
The Vela supernova exploded about
12,000 years ago roughly 800

33
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,160
light-years away from our Sun.

34
00:02:02,835 --> 00:02:08,260
Another explosion called Geminga
occurred roughly 300,000 years ago at

35
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:11,048
a distance of 550 light-years.

36
00:02:11,507 --> 00:02:13,540
The closest known white dwarf candidate,

37
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:17,760
IK Pegasi, is currently moving
away to a safe distance.

38
00:02:19,525 --> 00:02:21,880
If a blast wave cannot
destroy our atmosphere,

39
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:24,040
what makes a supernova dangerous?

40
00:02:24,999 --> 00:02:27,940
Supernovae act as a major
source of cosmic rays.

41
00:02:28,828 --> 00:02:31,840
They also emit fluxes of
X-rays and gamma rays.

42
00:02:32,607 --> 00:02:35,000
When gamma radiation hits
the upper atmosphere,

43
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:36,860
it causes chemical reactions.

44
00:02:37,904 --> 00:02:43,180
Radiolysis breaks apart these common
gas molecules and recombines them into

45
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:44,860
harmful nitrogen oxides.

46
00:02:45,579 --> 00:02:50,220
These newly created nitrogen oxides
notably deplete ozone molecules.

47
00:02:51,134 --> 00:02:56,060
In this way, atmospheric chemistry depletes
our planet's protective ozone layer.

48
00:02:56,766 --> 00:03:01,540
The atmosphere's protective ozone composition
is transformed by these reactions.

49
00:03:02,409 --> 00:03:06,140
Do invisible chemical reactions
occur instead of a shockwave?

50
00:03:07,861 --> 00:03:11,280
How close must a star be for
this lethal chemistry to occur?

51
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:17,460
An explosion within 33 light-years
happens on average only once every 240

52
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:18,760
million years.

53
00:03:19,551 --> 00:03:24,260
At this lethal distance, the incoming
radiation would destroy more than half of

54
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:25,440
Earth's ozone layer.

55
00:03:26,044 --> 00:03:31,420
Researchers derived this calculation from
atmospheric models and radiation data

56
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:34,740
from supernova 1987A.

57
00:03:35,342 --> 00:03:40,560
These events occur when an exploding white
dwarf releases tremendous energy within

58
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:41,360
mere seconds.

59
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:47,000
At peak brightness, such an explosion can
shine 5 billion times brighter than our

60
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:47,800
Sun.

61
00:03:47,921 --> 00:03:50,080
Because white dwarfs are dim and common,

62
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:53,700
they can detonate unpredictably
in unstudied systems.

63
00:03:54,430 --> 00:03:58,200
Does that distance boundary mark where
atmospheric protection collapses?

64
00:04:00,097 --> 00:04:04,280
What happens to the world below when
half of the ozone layer is wiped out?

65
00:04:04,930 --> 00:04:10,360
Ozone is the only atmospheric gas capable
of absorbing dangerous ultraviolet

66
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:12,260
B radiation from sunlight.

67
00:04:13,014 --> 00:04:15,020
When stratospheric ozone levels drop,

68
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:19,380
intense ultraviolet B light
penetrates directly to the surface.

69
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:23,620
This extreme radiation
damages cellular DNA,

70
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:28,360
causing pyrimidine bases to form
transcription errors during replication.

71
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:34,560
In humans and animals, excess
ultraviolet exposure causes severe skin

72
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:36,060
cancers and cataracts.

73
00:04:36,656 --> 00:04:41,900
Marine ecosystems suffer harm because
ultraviolet rays affect surface

74
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:42,820
and reef communities.

75
00:04:43,648 --> 00:04:48,260
Phytoplankton and reef communities are
particularly vulnerable to this elevated

76
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:49,060
radiation.

77
00:04:49,887 --> 00:04:54,340
Their collapse would seriously damage
the foundation of the ocean food web.

78
00:04:55,040 --> 00:05:00,400
At the same time, elevated cosmic rays
can alter climate patterns and cool

79
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:01,440
the global temperature.

80
00:05:02,245 --> 00:05:06,760
Gamma ray bursts from dangerously
close stars may have caused the ancient

81
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:07,840
end-Ordovician extinction.

82
00:05:08,875 --> 00:05:14,140
That prehistoric cataclysm resulted
in the death of nearly 60 per cent of

83
00:05:14,140 --> 00:05:15,420
all oceanic life.

84
00:05:16,126 --> 00:05:21,040
Rapid successive supernovae have also
been proposed as triggers for the Late

85
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:21,940
Devonian extinction.

86
00:05:23,881 --> 00:05:29,060
Did nearby exploding stars actually bombard
our planet during geological history?

87
00:05:29,635 --> 00:05:34,380
Proof of past supernovae was discovered
by examining deep seabed sediments.

88
00:05:34,984 --> 00:05:39,580
Dying stars forge heavy elements and
disperse them into space during their

89
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:41,040
catastrophic final moments.

90
00:05:41,795 --> 00:05:45,560
One isotope produced during
these events is iron-60,

91
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:49,260
which has a half-life
spanning 2.62 million years.

92
00:05:50,036 --> 00:05:54,840
They identified these stellar atoms within
the top two centimetres of the oceanic

93
00:05:54,840 --> 00:05:55,640
crust.

94
00:05:55,856 --> 00:06:00,820
This detection indicates an explosion
likely occurred within the last 5 million

95
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:01,620
years.

96
00:06:01,965 --> 00:06:07,220
Similar interstellar traces have also
appeared in Antarctic surface snow and in

97
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:08,020
lunar samples.

98
00:06:08,708 --> 00:06:13,420
Iron-60 atoms were even found
preserved inside fossilised bacteria

99
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:15,500
living in seabed sediments.

100
00:06:16,221 --> 00:06:20,520
So how close would a dying star need to
be to wipe out our protective atmospheric

101
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:21,320
shield?

102
00:06:21,846 --> 00:06:26,180
Gamma rays convert nitrogen and
oxygen into nitrogen oxides,

103
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:27,920
depleting the ozone layer.

104
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:31,700
What delicate balance protects
our planet from cosmic events?
